 @chenbrian834  It's not better. For starters, AMD needs more VRAM due to lack of optimization. On top of that, if you go with AMD, you don’t get DLSS or DLAA — which are literally better than native resolution. FSR 4 barely compares to DLSS 3. You also miss out on things like Multi-Frame Rendering, NVIDIA Reflex and Reflex 2, NVIDIA Studio, NVIDIA Video Scaling, and low power consumption — which is a whole other topic. The RTX 5060 has the raw power of a 3070, but the 3070 consumes 220W, while the 5060 only draws 145W.

In fact, the 9060 is the worst one. Sure, it may have the same raw power as the 5060 (and the RTX 3070, of course), but due to lack of optimization, its 8GB of GDDR6 VRAM just isn't enough. The 5060 also has 8GB, yeah… and it's limited by that, but not as much. The 3070 can still run games in 4K on ultra settings, even with ray tracing, and it also has 8GB.

What I do notice though, and this goes out to the hypocrites, is that no one’s saying a word about the 128-bit memory bus. Back when the 4060 came out with 128 bits, all of you were crying about it.

